{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R5"],
      "pins": ["R5.1", "R5.2"],
      "nets": ["/PWM_OUT", "PWM"],
      "observed": "R5 is a 0805 resistor placed in series between the MCU PWM net and the fan PWM connector pin, but its value is only 1 Ω: netlist shows (ref \"R5\") (value \"1\"), and the SPICE deck models it as R5 /PWM_OUT PWM 1.",
      "expected": "For a signal series resistor to a connector, standard practice is either a direct connection/0 Ω jumper or a current-limiting value comparable to the other signal series resistor R4, which is 1 kΩ. A 1 Ω resistor provides negligible protection and is inconsistent with its apparent role.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R5\") (value \"1\") (footprint \"Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric\")"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"2\") (name \"/PWM_OUT\") ... (node (ref \"R5\") (pin \"1\") ... (net (code \"10\") (name \"PWM\") ... (node (ref \"R5\") (pin \"2\")"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R5 /PWM_OUT PWM 1"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R4 TACH /TACH_IN 1k"}
      ],
      "impact": "The PWM line is essentially directly driven by the MCU. If the fan PWM input is shorted to a supply rail or has a strong pull-up, the MCU output and Q2 base drive can be overloaded; the resistor does not provide the expected signal-line protection.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The rails and main fan-control connectivity appear plausible, but R5 is a suspicious 1 Ω series resistor on the PWM signal path, unlike the comparable 1 kΩ tach series resistor and unlike standard protection practice."
}

